<p>Let <m>R</m> be the
rectangle  <m>3 \leq x \leq 3.4</m>,
<m>4 \leq y \leq 4.8</m>.  Find the values of Riemann
sums which are reasonable over- and under-estimates for
<m>\int_R f(x,y) \,dA</m> with <m>\Delta x=0.2</m> and <m>\Delta y=0.4</m>.</p>
<p>over-estimate: <fillin characters="25" name="AnSwEr0001"/></p>
<p>under-estimate: <fillin characters="25" name="AnSwEr0002"/></p>
</statement>
    <answer>
      <p>
        <m>46\cdot 0.2\cdot 0.4</m>
      </p>
    </answer>
    <answer>
      <p>
        <m>20\cdot 0.2\cdot 0.4</m>
      </p>
    </answer>
    <solution><image xmlns:pi="http://pretextbook.org/2020/pretext/internal" pi:generated="webwork/images/Library__Michigan__Chap16Sec1__Q01_pg-image-1.png" width="41%"/>




Then we can calculate the lower and upper sum by taking the smallest
and largest values on each rectangle:
<me>\mbox{Riemann sum} = \sum f(x_i,y_i)\Delta x\Delta y,</me>
so the lower sum is
<me>\mbox{Lower} = (5 + 7 + 3 + 5)(0.2)(0.4) = (20)(0.2)(0.4) = 1.6,</me>
and the upper sum is
<me>\mbox{Upper} = (9 + 12 + 7 + 18)(0.2)(0.4) = (46)(0.2)(0.4) = 3.68.</me>



</solution>
